In this lesson we'll look at how businesses use two straight-line models to figure out whether they're making money or losing it. First up, there's revenue, which is the money coming in. It usually equals the price you charge per item, multiplied by how many items you sell. Then there's cost, which is the money going out. Cost usually equals a variable cost per item, times the number of items, plus a fixed cost you have to pay no matter what, like rent. Next, profit and loss. If revenue is bigger than cost, you're in the black, meaning profit. If cost is bigger than revenue, you're in the red, meaning a loss. Then comes the key idea, the break-even point. That's where revenue equals cost exactly, so you're neither making nor losing money. You just set the two equations equal and solve for how many items you need to sell. Below that number, you're losing money; above it, you're making money. One quick caution though, the break-even number of items has to be a whole number you can actually sell, so always round up if your answer isn't a whole number. And finally, if you sketch both lines on the same graph, they cross right at break-even, with cost higher on the left and revenue higher on the right. Now let's see it in action.